# The working copy is Windows and the deploy target is Linux, so anything whose
# bytes are checked — rather than only its meaning — has to be pinned to one
# line ending or it will differ between the two machines while being the same
# file.
#
# Migrations are the case that actually bit: db/migrate.js records a sha256 of
# each applied migration to catch someone editing a file that already ran, and
# a CRLF/LF flip made an untouched migration look edited, which blocked every
# later migration. migrate.js now normalises before hashing, but pinning the
# files here fixes it at the source instead of working around it downstream.
*.sql           text eol=lf
*.json          text eol=lf
*.js            text eol=lf
*.mjs           text eol=lf
*.css           text eol=lf
*.html          text eol=lf
*.md            text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf

# Binary — never touch these.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
